+## Small Macs
+
+16 GB Macs run the existing 4-bit checkpoint. No pack ships for 8 GB Macs: the only recipes that fit, at 2 bits,
+lose the task on code and reasoning, and a stock Qwen3.5-9B converted the same way loses it just as badly.
+
+| tier | recipe | ships | disk | 4k | 8k | 32k |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 16 GB | `q4` (no recipe) | **yes** | 5.07 GB | 7.60 GiB | 7.82 GiB | 9.13 GiB |
+| 16 GB | `s16.json` | no: q4 matches its quality at twice the prefill | 5.70 GB | 8.22 GiB | 8.43 GiB | 9.75 GiB |
+| 8 GB | `s8-b.json` | no: wrong code and reasoning | 3.23 GB | 4.65 GiB | refused | refused |
+| 8 GB | `s8-d.json` | no: repeats itself on code | 3.02 GB | 4.45 GiB | 4.67 GiB | refused |
+
+Memory is the peak process footprint (`tensorfold:process_footprint_peak_bytes`) after one request of about half the
+context. Each run uses `--parallel 1 --prompt-cache-gib 0 --no-drafts`, with the working set capped by
+`TF_QWEN27_WORKING_SET_BYTES` to what Metal grants a 16 GB Mac (11453251584) or an 8 GB Mac (5726623744). The 16 GB
+runs pick 512 prompt rows; the 8 GB runs pick 128. RSS stays flat per pack (q4 5.07, s16 5.68, s8-b 3.36,
+s8-d 3.17 GiB) because the KV and prompt scratch are GPU-private. "refused" means startup refuses the context
+beside the weights and the margin. The targets are about 10.5 GB at 8k and about 5.0 GB at 4k. Both 16 GB packs
+are well under the target, and both 8 GB packs fit at 4k.
+
+### Convert
+
+```bash
+python3 tools/sensenova_loop_mlx.py --source SenseNova-Loop-9B --out loop9b-mlx4
+python3 tools/sensenova_loop_mlx.py --source SenseNova-Loop-9B --out loop9b-s16 \
+  --recipe docs/small-quant-recipes/s16.json --clip-search 10
+```
+
+`docs/small-quant-candidates.md` lists each recipe's per-module widths. The same command converts stock
+`Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B` for the control.
+
+### Quality
+
+Each figure is teacher-forced top-1 agreement over 64 positions on the three fixed prompts (prose, code,
+reasoning), against each model's own BF16 continuation. The SenseNova reference comes from transformers. The
+control's reference comes from stock BF16 run greedily through transformers on MPS.
+
+| recipe | SenseNova vs BF16 | SenseNova vs q4 | stock Qwen3.5-9B vs BF16 |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `q4` | 57 / 62 / 61 | - | 64 / 64 / 58 |
+| `s16` | 58 / 61 / 62 | 57 / 56 / 62 | - |
+| `s8-b` | 50 / 44 / 53 | 55 / 36 / 54 | 64 / 44 / 44 |
+| `s8-d` | 52 / 38 / 47 | 48 / 25 / 47 | 61 / 45 / 44 |
+
+Prose / code / reasoning, out of 64. At 2 bits the loss on code and reasoning matches the control's, so it is normal
+2-bit loss, not something the loop adds. It is still a collapse. Free-running greedy on `def fib(n): ... return`
+gives `fib(n-1)` with unclosed calls (`s8-b`) or `None` and the function repeated (`s8-d`, and the stock `s8-b`).
+On the train question, `s8-b` invents answer choices. 3 bits fits neither tier: about 3.9 GB of weights plus about
+1.85 GiB of 4k overhead is over the 8 GB budget. `s16` adds nothing over q4 that the gate can resolve.
+
+### Speed
+
+Measured on the M5 Ultra at its own working set. Prefill is cold CLI prefill (`tf-qwen27-run`, chunk 2048).
+TTFT is a served streaming request that follows a short unrelated one, with `--context 33024 --parallel 1`.
+Decode is a served stream of 256 tokens. Each is 2 repetitions, which agree to 0.5% for prefill and TTFT and to
+4% for decode.
+
+| recipe | prefill 4k / 8k / 32k tok/s | TTFT 4k / 8k / 32k | decode tok/s |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `q4` | 2754 / 2719 / 2465 | 1.50 / 3.04 / 13.35 s | 58 |
+| `s16` | 1409 / 1394 / 1327 | 2.89 / 5.81 / 24.47 s | 49 |
+| `s8-b` | 844 / 839 / 815 | 4.84 / 9.70 / 40.07 s | 46 |
+| `s8-d` | 836 / 833 / 808 | 4.91 / 9.85 / 40.58 s | 45 |
+
+Only 4-bit group-64 weights take the tuned prompt and decode kernels (`projection.zig`, `quant_gpu.zig`). Every
+other width falls back to the general path, so fewer bits run slower. A fast 2- or 3-bit prompt kernel would be
+needed before a smaller pack could win on speed. That still would not fix the quality loss above.
+
 ## What is not covered
 
 - video: the processor's video path is not wired, and video parts are refused.
